## REASONING

**Decomposition.** The request has two coupled goals: (1) a *mechanistic* goal — quantify how elevation controls the day-of-year (DOY) of snowmelt onset — and (2) an implicit *validation* goal — do this "at each individual SNOTEL station location," i.e. anchored to the 6 SNOTEL SWE records. There is also a sharpened sub-goal: "resolve the local lapse rate between stations on the same ridge," where some stations are **<12 km apart**.

**Feasibility — the crux.** Snowmelt onset is derivable from ELM's default snow output (H2OSNO, QSNOMELT, SNOW), and SNOTEL SWE is available in-domain, so melt-timing *validation* is possible. Snow is a fast state, so multi-year spin-up is not required. That part is sound.

But the **primary mechanism as posed is only partly representable**. Melt onset is driven overwhelmingly by the forcing (temperature/radiation seasonality, snowfall accumulation), and the default forcing is NLDAS-2 at 0.125° (~12 km). Two facts collide:

- Across the *full* Naches relief (352–1977 m, ~1625 m), NLDAS *does* deliver orographically-varying precip/temperature across cells — so a **coarse elevation gradient in melt onset across stations separated by >12 km is answerable.**
- **Stations <12 km apart fall in (or share) the same NLDAS cell.** There is no within-cell lapse rate. Two stations on the same ridge separated by a few hundred meters of elevation but <12 km horizontally will receive **near-identical forcing**, so ELM cannot produce a physically-driven melt-onset difference between them. Any difference would be a soil artifact, not a lapse-rate signal. **"Resolving the local lapse rate between stations on the same ridge" is therefore NOT answerable with the current stack** — it needs elevation-downscaled (sub-12 km) forcing.

Expect additional ridgetop SWE underestimation because a 12 km cell averages over terrain — this is a known limit and is itself a reportable result.

**Design.** This is inherently observation-pinned: N is dictated by the 6 SNOTEL stations, not chosen freely. I place one validation column at each station. I add a small exploratory transect (~4 columns) spanning distinct NLDAS elevation bands to characterize the *forcing-resolved* elevation→melt-onset gradient that the stack CAN represent, cleanly separated from the sub-grid lapse-rate question it cannot. I cannot enumerate concrete lat/lon because the brief does not provide SNOTEL station coordinates; fabricating them would be wrong. I flag a SNOTEL-metadata resolver under requires_capabilities.

Verdict: **partial** — melt-onset validation and the coarse (>12 km) elevation gradient are feasible; the same-ridge local lapse rate is infeasible without downscaled forcing.

